Using Serilog, you do not need to add logging statements manually to every part of your application unless specific, detailed tracing of values or states is needed. The initial setup and strategic use of middleware for request logging can automatically capture a lot of useful information with little to no modification to the rest of your code. This setup not only minimizes the intrusion into your business logic but also maintains clean and maintainable code.

When using Serilog in an ASP.NET Core application, it's crucial to consider how you will handle and log exceptions. Serilog provides powerful capabilities for logging exceptions, but integrating these capabilities into your application to ensure that all errors are captured and logged appropriately often requires more than just configuring the logger. Here are some strategies you can use to ensure comprehensive error trapping and logging with Serilog:

### 1. **Middleware for Error Logging**
ASP.NET Core's middleware pipeline is an effective way to catch and log exceptions globally. If you haven't already set up exception handling middleware, here’s how you can do it alongside Serilog to ensure all unhandled exceptions are logged:

```csharp
